      <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          Static vs Dynamic QR Codes: Do Free QR Codes Expire?
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          A common frustration among online users is printing thousands of flyers only to find out months later that their QR code suddenly stopped working, showing a "Subscription Expired" notice. It is important to understand the fundamental technical difference between static and dynamic codes:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 pt-1">
          
          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 flex items-center gap-1.5">
              <span>✓</span> Static QR Codes (What This Tool Creates)
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
              In a static QR code, the full alphanumeric destination (such as <code>https://yourdomain.com</code> or your Wi-Fi password) is directly mathematically encoded into the geometric pixel grid.
            </p>
            <ul class="text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 space-y-1 list-disc list-inside pt-1">
              <li><strong>Zero expiration risk:</strong> Works forever.</li>
              <li><strong>Zero dependencies:</strong> Scanners decode the data directly with no server middleman.</li>
              <li><strong>True privacy:</strong> Nobody can intercept or monitor your visitor traffic.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="p-5 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-white dark:bg-slate-900 space-y-2">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400 flex items-center gap-1.5">
              <span>⚠️</span> Dynamic QR Codes (Subscription Redirection)
            </h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
              Dynamic QR codes encode a short redirect link owned by a commercial service (e.g., <code>qr.co/abc123</code>). When scanned, the provider looks up your real URL and forwards the visitor.
            </p>
            <ul class="text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 space-y-1 list-disc list-inside pt-1">
              <li><strong>Recurring cost:</strong> If you stop paying their monthly subscription, the link breaks.</li>
              <li><strong>Redirect latency:</strong> Adds an unnecessary network hop before landing on your page.</li>
              <li><strong>Single point of failure:</strong> If the provider shuts down, your print runs are ruined.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- SECTION 6: PRINTING BEST PRACTICES -->
