        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white tracking-tight">
          Best Practices for Printing and Scanning QR Codes
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          To ensure your printed QR codes can be read quickly and reliably across all smartphone models and lighting conditions, adhere to the following professional design principles:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 pt-1">
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-900/40 space-y-1.5">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">1. Maintain Strong Color Contrast</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
              Optical sensors detect light reflection. Always ensure the foreground barcode modules are significantly darker than the background. Dark charcoal, deep navy, or classic black on a crisp white or light ivory background provides maximum scanning speed.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-900/40 space-y-1.5">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">2. Respect the Quiet Zone Margin</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
              The white border framing the QR code is technically called the "Quiet Zone." It isolates the matrix from surrounding text and imagery. Maintain at least 2 to 4 modules of margin on all four sides so camera firmware can locate the three corner position squares.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-900/40 space-y-1.5">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">3. Minimum Physical Print Sizing</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
              For business cards and tabletop cards scanned within arm's reach (0.5m), print the code at least <strong>2 cm × 2 cm (0.8 × 0.8 inches)</strong>. For window decals, posters, or exhibition stands viewed from 2 meters away, increase dimensions to at least 20 cm × 20 cm.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-200 dark:border-slate-800 bg-slate-50 dark:bg-slate-900/40 space-y-1.5">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">4. Select High Error Correction for Logos</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
              Reed-Solomon error correction allows cameras to reconstruct missing or obstructed data. If placing a logo in the center or printing on items susceptible to scratching (stickers, outdoor signage), always choose Level <strong>Q (25%)</strong> or <strong>H (30%)</strong>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

